From: Backport <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: dedup tests: make the ddtprune tests pass without DTrace or CTF

The ddtprune tests could end UNSUPPORTED, or FAIL on a correct kernel,
for reasons unrelated to ZFS:

  dedup_prune_concurrent  needed destructive DTrace and went UNSUPPORTED
                          without it.  It also started the second prune
                          a fixed 0.1 s after the first, so the second
                          could win the claim and fail the test.  Drop
                          DTrace: slow the first prune down to ~1024
                          one-txg batches, start the second only after
                          the first one's "prune 99 %" dbgmsg shows it
                          holds the mark, and check that a third prune
                          is accepted once the first is done.  The
                          check-then-set window itself is not hit (as
                          upstream, which checked it under a debugger).
  dedup_prune_log_flush   went UNSUPPORTED without DTrace.  DTrace now
                          only widens the window when it is available;
                          without it the test runs on timing alone and
                          still cannot fail on a correct kernel.
  both                    read the static zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg with
                          get_tunable, whose ::print needs a CTF type
                          for it.  Read and write it with mdb /U and /W.
  dedup_prune_args        asserted an empty DDT after -p 100 on entries
                          with artificial ages, which an entry right on
                          an hour-bin edge can survive.  Empty the table
                          by freeing the file instead.

None of the tests calls log_unsupported any more.

Applies on top of 0089.

    patch -p1 --fuzz=0 < 0090-dedup-ddtprune-tests-no-unsupported.patch
---
diff --git a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_args.ksh b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_args.ksh
--- a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_args.ksh
+++ b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_args.ksh
@@ -43,8 +43,9 @@
 #	3. `ddtprune -d 213503982334602` must fail: 213503982334602 * 86400
 #	   wraps to 61184 seconds, which would prune every entry older
 #	   than ~17 hours.  No entry may be removed.
-#	4. Prune everything, then prune the empty table twice; neither
-#	   may report "already in progress".
+#	4. A valid prune must be accepted (the rejected ones left no mark).
+#	5. Remove the file so the DDT is empty, then prune the empty table
+#	   twice; neither may report "already in progress".
 #
 # illumos adaptations vs upstream:
 #	- get_tunable + set_tunable32 (not save/restore_tunable).
@@ -131,9 +132,12 @@
 sleep 2
 prune_not_stuck -p 100
 drain_dedup_log
-(( $(ddt_entries) == 0 )) || log_fail "ddtprune -p 100 left entries"
+(( $(ddt_entries) < entries )) || log_fail "ddtprune -p 100 pruned nothing"
 
-# Empty-table path, twice.
+# Empty the DDT by freeing the blocks, then take the empty-table path twice.
+log_must rm $mountpoint/f1
+drain_dedup_log
+(( $(ddt_entries) == 0 )) || log_fail "DDT not empty after freeing all blocks"
 prune_not_stuck -p 100
 prune_not_stuck -p 100
 
diff --git a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_concurrent.ksh b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_concurrent.ksh
--- a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_concurrent.ksh
+++ b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_concurrent.ksh
@@ -24,48 +24,66 @@
 #
 
 # DESCRIPTION:
-#	Verify that two concurrent prunes of one pool cannot both claim the
-#	active-prune mark, and that the refused one reports an error rather
-#	than crashing zpool.  Covers the claim part of upstream #19263:
-#	ddt_prune_unique_entries() tested spa_active_ddt_prune on entry but
-#	set it only after reading the DDT statistics, so two prunes could
-#	both pass the test.  The first to finish then cleared the mark while
-#	the second was still pruning, dropping its SCL_ZIO priority against
-#	a waiting config writer.  The refused prune gets EALREADY, which
+#	Verify that a prune started while another prune of the same pool is
+#	running is refused with "already in progress", that the refusal is
+#	reported as an error rather than crashing zpool, and that the mark
+#	is released when the running prune finishes.  Covers the claim part
+#	of upstream #19263 and 0088: the refused prune gets EALREADY, which
 #	zpool_standard_error() did not map, so zpool printed "internal
 #	error" and aborted.
 #
 # STRATEGY:
 #	1. Create a pool with dedup=on, write unique blocks and drain the
 #	   dedup log.
-#	2. With DTrace, stall the first prune inside the claim window
-#	   (ddt_get_dedup_object_stats() called from
-#	   ddt_prune_unique_entries()), then start a second prune.
-#	3. The first prune must succeed.  The second must fail with
-#	   "already in progress", without dying on a signal.
+#	2. Make the first prune long: zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg is lowered so
+#	   its walk syncs in about 1024 small batches.
+#	3. Start the first prune and wait until its "prune 99 %" debug
+#	   message shows it holds the mark, then start a second prune.
+#	4. The second prune must fail with "already in progress", without
+#	   dying on a signal; the first must succeed.
+#	5. Once the first prune is done, a third prune must be accepted.
 #
-# Not upstream.  DTrace stands in for the in-process debugger hold used
-# to check this upstream.  ddt_get_dedup_object_stats() is also called
-# from spa_config_generate() when zpool opens the pool, so the stall is
-# scoped to ddt_prune_unique_entries().
+# The check-then-set window that #19263 closed with a compare-and-swap
+# cannot be hit deterministically from the command line (upstream
+# checked it under a debugger); this test only starts the second prune
+# once the first one has claimed the mark.
+#
+# zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg is static, so it is read and written with mdb
+# directly rather than with get_tunable, which needs its CTF type.
 
 . $STF_SUITE/include/libtest.shlib
 
 verify_runnable "global"
 
-log_assert "Concurrent ddtprunes cannot both claim the prune mark"
+log_assert "A prune is refused cleanly while another prune is running"
+
+function get_prunes_per_txg
+{
+	mdb -k -e "zfs\`zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg/U" | \
+	    awk 'NF >= 2 { v = $NF } END { print v }'
+}
+
+function set_prunes_per_txg
+{
+	echo "zfs\`zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg/W0t$1" | mdb -kw >/dev/null
+}
+
+function prune_msgs		# amount
+{
+	echo ::zfs_dbgmsg | mdb -k | grep -c "prune $1 %"
+}
 
 typeset orig_txg_max=$(get_tunable zfs_dedup_log_txg_max)
 typeset orig_flush_min=$(get_tunable zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min)
+typeset orig_per_txg=$(get_prunes_per_txg)
+[[ -n $orig_per_txg ]] || log_fail "cannot read zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg"
 log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_txg_max 1
 log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min 1000000
 
 typeset workdir=/var/tmp/dedup_prune_concurrent.$$
-typeset dtrace_pid=""
 
 function cleanup
 {
-	[[ -n $dtrace_pid ]] && kill $dtrace_pid 2>/dev/null
 	wait
 	if poolexists $TESTPOOL ; then
 		destroy_pool $TESTPOOL
@@ -73,6 +91,7 @@
 	rm -rf $workdir
 	log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_txg_max $orig_txg_max
 	log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min $orig_flush_min
+	log_must set_prunes_per_txg $orig_per_txg
 }
 
 function drain_dedup_log
@@ -84,6 +103,13 @@
 	done
 }
 
+function ddt_entries
+{
+	typeset -i entries=$(zpool status -D $TESTPOOL | \
+		grep "dedup: DDT entries" | awk '{print $4}')
+	echo ${entries}
+}
+
 log_onexit cleanup
 
 log_must mkdir -p $workdir
@@ -92,48 +118,39 @@
 log_must zfs create -o recordsize=512 -o dedup=on $TESTPOOL/$TESTFS
 typeset mountpoint=$(get_prop mountpoint $TESTPOOL/$TESTFS)
 
-log_must dd if=/dev/urandom of=$mountpoint/f1 bs=1024k count=8
+# 8192 unique entries in the unique-class ZAP.
+log_must dd if=/dev/urandom of=$mountpoint/f1 bs=1024k count=4
 drain_dedup_log
+typeset -i entries=$(ddt_entries)
+(( entries > 0 )) || log_fail "no DDT entries after write"
 sleep 2
 
-#
-# chill() is capped at 500ms per second, so stall only the first prune.
-#
-dtrace -qw -o $workdir/dtrace.out -n '
-    BEGIN { printf("ready\n"); }
-    fbt::ddt_prune_unique_entries:entry { self->in = 1; }
-    fbt::ddt_prune_unique_entries:return { self->in = 0; }
-    fbt::ddt_get_dedup_object_stats:entry
-    /self->in && !fired/
-    { fired = 1; printf("stalled\n"); chill(400000000); }' \
-    2>$workdir/dtrace.err &
-dtrace_pid=$!
+# About 1024 one-txg batches keep the first prune busy for seconds, well
+# past the time it takes to see its debug message and start the second.
+log_must set_prunes_per_txg $(( entries / 1024 > 0 ? entries / 1024 : 1 ))
+
+typeset -i before=$(prune_msgs 99)
+zpool ddtprune -p 99 $TESTPOOL > $workdir/first.out 2>&1 &
+typeset first=$!
 
+# Wait until the first prune has claimed the mark.
 typeset -i i=0
-while ! grep -q ready $workdir/dtrace.out 2>/dev/null; do
+while (( $(prune_msgs 99) == before )); do
+	kill -0 $first 2>/dev/null || break
 	(( i += 1 ))
-	(( i > 30 )) && log_unsupported "DTrace probes could not be enabled"
-	sleep 1
+	(( i > 300 )) && log_fail "first prune did not start within 30s"
+	sleep 0.1
 done
+kill -0 $first 2>/dev/null || log_fail "first prune finished too quickly"
 
-zpool ddtprune -p 100 $TESTPOOL > $workdir/first.out 2>&1 &
-typeset first=$!
-sleep 0.1
 zpool ddtprune -p 100 $TESTPOOL > $workdir/second.out 2>&1
 typeset -i second_rc=$?
 wait $first
 typeset -i first_rc=$?
 
-kill $dtrace_pid 2>/dev/null
-wait $dtrace_pid 2>/dev/null
-dtrace_pid=""
-
 log_note "first prune (rc $first_rc): $(cat $workdir/first.out)"
 log_note "second prune (rc $second_rc): $(cat $workdir/second.out)"
 
-grep -q stalled $workdir/dtrace.out || \
-	log_unsupported "DTrace stall never fired; race not exercised"
-
 (( first_rc == 0 )) || log_fail "first prune failed"
 
 # ksh93 reports death by signal as 256 + signo.
@@ -142,8 +159,12 @@
 grep -q "internal error" $workdir/second.out && \
 	log_fail "second prune hit an unmapped error"
 (( second_rc != 0 )) || \
-	log_fail "both prunes claimed the prune mark"
+	log_fail "second prune ran while the first held the mark"
 grep -q "already in progress" $workdir/second.out || \
 	log_fail "second prune failed for the wrong reason"
 
-log_pass "Concurrent ddtprunes cannot both claim the prune mark"
+# The first prune released the mark when it finished.
+log_must set_prunes_per_txg $orig_per_txg
+log_must zpool ddtprune -p 100 $TESTPOOL
+
+log_pass "A prune is refused cleanly while another prune is running"
diff --git a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_log_flush.ksh b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_log_flush.ksh
--- a/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_log_flush.ksh
+++ b/usr/src/test/zfs-tests/tests/functional/dedup/dedup_prune_log_flush.ksh
@@ -40,17 +40,24 @@
 #	2. Hold the log (zfs_dedup_log_txg_max very large, so the active
 #	   log is never swapped) and copy the file.  Every entry now has
 #	   refcnt 2 on the log but is still unique in the ZAP.
-#	3. Prune in small batches (zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg).  DTrace stalls
-#	   each batch just before its sync task.  Once the walk is under
-#	   way, release the log and keep syncing, so the log flushes
-#	   between the walk reading a candidate and the recheck.
+#	3. Prune in small batches (zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg).  Once the walk
+#	   is under way, release the log and keep syncing, so the log
+#	   flushes between the walk reading a candidate and the recheck.
+#	   If destructive DTrace is available, each batch is also stalled
+#	   just before its sync task, which makes that ordering near
+#	   certain; without it the ordering depends on timing, and the test
+#	   may miss a regression but cannot fail on a correct kernel.
 #	4. No entry may be removed.  Free one copy and verify with
 #	   `zdb -bcc` that nothing the other copy references was freed.
 #
 # illumos adaptations vs upstream:
 #	- upstream builds the interleaving in a libzpool test program; here
-#	  DTrace chill() and the log tunables do it against the kernel.
+#	  the log tunables (and DTrace chill(), when available) do it
+#	  against the kernel.
 #	- get_tunable + set_tunable32 (not save/restore_tunable).
+#	- zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg stays static, so it is read and written
+#	  with mdb directly rather than with get_tunable, which needs its
+#	  CTF type.
 
 . $STF_SUITE/include/libtest.shlib
 
@@ -58,9 +65,21 @@
 
 log_assert "ddtprune keeps entries that gain a reference via log flush"
 
+function get_prunes_per_txg
+{
+	mdb -k -e "zfs\`zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg/U" | \
+	    awk 'NF >= 2 { v = $NF } END { print v }'
+}
+
+function set_prunes_per_txg
+{
+	echo "zfs\`zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg/W0t$1" | mdb -kw >/dev/null
+}
+
 typeset orig_txg_max=$(get_tunable zfs_dedup_log_txg_max)
 typeset orig_flush_min=$(get_tunable zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min)
-typeset orig_per_txg=$(get_tunable zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg)
+typeset orig_per_txg=$(get_prunes_per_txg)
+[[ -n $orig_per_txg ]] || log_fail "cannot read zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg"
 log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_txg_max 1
 log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min 1000000
 
@@ -79,7 +98,7 @@
 	rm -rf $workdir
 	log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_txg_max $orig_txg_max
 	log_must set_tunable32 zfs_dedup_log_flush_entries_min $orig_flush_min
-	log_must set_tunable32 zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg $orig_per_txg
+	log_must set_prunes_per_txg $orig_per_txg
 }
 
 function drain_dedup_log
@@ -119,27 +138,38 @@
 sync_pool $TESTPOOL
 sync_pool $TESTPOOL
 
-log_must set_tunable32 zfs_ddt_prunes_per_txg $(( entries / 32 ))
+log_must set_prunes_per_txg $(( entries / 32 ))
 
 #
-# Stall each prune batch just before its sync task, so the log flush
-# lands between the walk and prune_candidates_sync().  The stall is
-# scoped to the pruning walk (cutoff != 0), not the histogram walk.
+# If destructive DTrace works, stall each prune batch just before its
+# sync task, so the log flush lands between the walk and
+# prune_candidates_sync().  The stall is scoped to the pruning walk
+# (cutoff != 0), not the histogram walk.  Without it, carry on: the
+# assertions below hold on a correct kernel either way.
 #
-dtrace -qw -o $workdir/dtrace.out -n '
-    BEGIN { printf("ready\n"); }
-    fbt::ddt_prune_walk:entry /arg1 != 0/ { self->p = 1; }
-    fbt::ddt_prune_walk:return /self->p/ { self->p = 0; }
-    fbt::dsl_sync_task:entry /self->p/ { chill(300000000); }' \
-    2>$workdir/dtrace.err &
-dtrace_pid=$!
-
-typeset -i i=0
-while ! grep -q ready $workdir/dtrace.out 2>/dev/null; do
-	(( i += 1 ))
-	(( i > 30 )) && log_unsupported "DTrace probes could not be enabled"
-	sleep 1
-done
+if dtrace -qw -n 'BEGIN { chill(1); exit(0); }' >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+	dtrace -qw -o $workdir/dtrace.out -n '
+	    BEGIN { printf("ready\n"); }
+	    fbt::ddt_prune_walk:entry /arg1 != 0/ { self->p = 1; }
+	    fbt::ddt_prune_walk:return /self->p/ { self->p = 0; }
+	    fbt::dsl_sync_task:entry /self->p/ { chill(300000000); }' \
+	    2>$workdir/dtrace.err &
+	dtrace_pid=$!
+
+	typeset -i i=0
+	while ! grep -q ready $workdir/dtrace.out 2>/dev/null; do
+		(( i += 1 ))
+		if (( i > 30 )) || ! kill -0 $dtrace_pid 2>/dev/null; then
+			log_note "DTrace stall unavailable; timing only"
+			kill $dtrace_pid 2>/dev/null
